A pending regulatory or legislative change: a legal measure from a government body (legislature, executive, agency or regulator, at any level and in any jurisdiction) that, as of 2026-11-01, is either (i) proposed but not final, for example a proposed rule, an introduced or pending bill, or a draft order, or (ii) final but not yet in effect. It is in the class only if its direct legal effect would change what a business pays for one of the following.

(a) Labor: wages and salaries, including minimum wages; overtime pay or eligibility; required paid leave or other mandated benefits; payroll taxes or employer contributions; scheduling, staffing-level or worker-classification rules that change the hours, pay or headcount the business must provide.

(b) Inputs: the price or availability of goods, materials, energy, services or other things the business buys to produce what it sells, for example tariffs, duties or quotas; excise or sales taxes on inputs; and product, energy or environmental standards that raise the price of inputs or limit which inputs can be bought.

Out of the class:
- measures already in effect as of 2026-11-01;
- court decisions;
- measures whose cost effect is only general compliance, reporting or paperwork burden, unless the item says the measure itself requires extra paid labor or purchased inputs;
- measures that act only on the business's revenue, demand, financing or income taxes.
